  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Supporting gender interventions in East Africa Programmes</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender Environment and Sustainable Development (GESD) is a National NGO, whose main objective is to enhance and facilitate the socio-economic empowerment of local communities as well as enable them to realize their socio-economic potentials through sustainable use of their environment and increased food security while mainstreaming gender and health factors in all programs. 

CAFOD in partnership with GESD has supported Catholic Diocesan Gender Mainstreaming Programme (CDGP) for the past five years. This programme comes to an end by end of July 2017. 

Gender Environment and Sustainable Development (GESD) has built on key outcomes of gender audit and mentored CAFOD Implementing partners to mainstream gender in their projects. Key objectives was to analyse the level of gender awareness and inclusiveness in programmes for the Catholic Dioceses of Kitui,  Maralal, Isiolo and the Archdiocese of Mombasa.  Ways in which gender programmes and projects can be integrated in mainstream programmes at the diocesan level have since been documented; and studies conducted to recommend ways in which community mechanisms can tackle gender based violence and gender stereotypes with respect to specific social cultural circumstances in the dioceses.  The extent to which mainstreaming gender in programmes and ensuring vulnerable groups are protected with regard to gender based violence is now evidenced in projects of the supported Implementing partners. 

This final approval therefore seeks to support GESD to build a cohesive, comprehensive and sustainable  exit strategy that will support key gender mainstreaming activities at diocesan level and at GESD level as an organization. The wrap up will ensure GESD efficiently manages community based activities and plans for a responsible organisational programme closure prior to CAFOD phase out scheduled for October 2017</narrative>
  </description>
